a  neighboring town indicating to us that  she isn't even in the castle they are  hiking towards it creates a wonderful  sense of tension on top of the already  tense situation created by the two  clamming up there in the first place and  what's funny is I remember sitting down  as I was reading about Luffy and Sanji  riding a tree down an avalanche  surrounded by Giants snowboarding bodies  and thought this wasn't at all what I  was expecting in the best possible of  ways however as things  into more trouble as they often do in  this series the tenacious claim that  Luffy goes on with both Sanjay and Nami  on his back up the mountain unassisted  was by far the most Luffy thing I've  seen and when he arrives on top before  collapsing to the floor I genuinely shed  a tear because he loves his friends that  much that he would kill himself to do  this and despite being on the verge of  collapse hypothermia and exhaustion  Luffy says to the person that finds them  a top of the peak my friends they're my  friends he says this after climbing at  15,000 foot mountain with nothing left  and he would assume in need for help  himself yet he only draws attention to  them it makes me wonder why and how  loyalty has become such a massive deal  for Luffy to an almost absurd degree -  it makes me wonder more about his origin  and what happened to his family in  addition to that this arc had some of  the absolute funniest gags in the series  so far on more than a few occasions I  found myself wheezing with laughter far  more than I even had a little garden  stuff like Zorro starting a fight with  some random solider in the name of  justice but really it's the steel his  warm clothing really got me but the  funniest guy in the entire arc for me  came as princess Vivi in an attempt to  wake up a fading goose up in the snow  slaps the ever-loving daylights out of  his face causing it to swell up a crazy  amount which on its own is funny already  but when Zorro stumbles upon the two he  greets princess baby and stares long and  hard at this swollen face of Aesop's  before recognizing his nose and calling  him by his name I love stuff like that  it's just great character interactions  also worth mentioning the way this arc  ends is both insanely interesting and  raises so many damn questions my head  hurts for gold Rogers name to actually  be Goldie Roger is a friggin awesome  swerve I didn't see coming and makes me  wonder what his connection to monkey dee  Luffy is seen as they seem to be the  only characters with that sort of name  compounding this we are greeted by this  new character called ace that seems to  know Luffy and during some conference  some years ago during Vivi's childhood  flashback we got to see a poster of that  guy from low tide who controlled the  weather this is such a bizarre intro  detailed story and I have no idea what's  to come next in alabaster but I'm really  excited however that isn't all I want to  discuss with regards drum Island as  there are certain aspects I did dislike  in it you see a large part of what makes  shown it as popular as it is is the  empowerment the reader gets to  experience or read about when characters  like Goku Naruto and Luffy restore  balance our justice in the worlds they  operate within also known as beating up  the villains in a lot of ways this  gut-punch Goku eventually gives to  Frieza is cathartic and makes me feel so  damn good  because Frieza is fantastic at being a  villain and this is just one example  there are tons across anime and even one  piece like Ireland don krieg buggy that  even the baroque works i've just been  introduced to heck see luffy deck Arland  that very first plane gave me shivers  but in this act a large portion of the  conflict in tension after Nami recovers  depends on this villain character King  wopo who really for all intents and  purposes is the most overly cartoonish  and least interesting villain I've seen  in one piece so far every scene he's a  this sort of annoyed me and bored me  because of how overly simplistic and how  obvious every decision he made was to  predict unlike Creed he has no once on  like buggy he has no charisma and unlike  our LAN we don't have that strong a  connection to the character he's  threatening just yet in chopper or at  least I didn't yet the only reason I  cared even a little bit about his ending  was entirely down to how much I wanted  chopper to have a happy ending for  himself because I do like the little  guys character and story and while the  ending fight seat had some epic and  totally boss Luffy moments with him  protecting the skull and crossbones on  the tower Apple also might be the  easiest main village fight Luffy has  fought so far in the entire series  but by firing away the single most  interesting thing to ever come out of  this act for me was a scene that caused  me more arguing and confusion than any  other scene I have ever had the pleasure  of reading and that scene was dr. kitty  Luke's flashback sequence showing  Choppers backstory this was a friggin  roller coaster of discovery for me and  I'm not talking about the narrative let  me explain upon reading this sequence in  the manga I really and I mean really  didn't like dr. Harry Luke's character  which is an issue because as it turns  out I am supposed to connect with this  character and think him to be a good  person but I found him to be a veiny AK  for a multitude of reasons and while he  certainly grew and me more as the  flashback continued I never could really  get past that bad taste my mouth when I  saw the character on the page and so  after finishing the arc I explained my  feelings about this story to AJ and in  response he edited out that very  30-minute flashback scene from the drum  Island movie to show me stating that he  thought I got the wrong impression and  so I watched the animated version and  boy is it different in fact it was so  different that I actually ended up  really liking the doctors character in  the movie version and even the anime  version on crunchyroll so much more than  I did in the manga which naturally led  me to bawling my eyes out at little  choppers unfortunate and tragic story  but what was so different about these  versions well quite a lot actually for  instance this is how the movie version  of events plays out it begins as dr.  Hilu Luke's running from the police and  meets dr. kou daija would have the only  other doctors in the place on the bridge  that's how it starts but did you know  that there's a large portion cut from  the original that happens before this  for instance the anime version on  crunchyroll shows a bit more but again I  still sort of liked it in that version  the flashback opens as soldiers  explained to us that by the order of the  king dr. Hedrick is to be captured this  immediately paints him as a sympathetic  character as we all  oh that we hate the king we then cut to  inside a family home where through their  own dialogue they explained the father  desperately needs a doctor but all the  doctors in the area are gone  this creates urgency and paints dr.  Hannah Duke as a courageous character  that is taking his life into his own  hands to protect those around him that's  when dr. healer Duke shows up and tries  to help the family despite being a  terrible doctor against the family's  wishes the mother ends up running to  report him and he makes the dad act like  a frog no big deal and it's sort of  funny he then flees the home not  breaking anything before meeting dr.  Cudahy on the bridge like he does where  the movie version picks up already you  noticed a substantial difference between  the movie and the anime right well  that's nothing compared to my experience  reading the manga bearing in mind that  this was my very first exposure to the  character and scene and I am supposed to  care and like this character for the  flashback to be effective alright here  it is  in the manga the scene opens dogs are  barking with a shadowy figure running  through the snow there's commotion in  the town square apparently here Duke has  burned down at least one home and either  robbed that one or another for money  there is nothing said about the Kings  desire to take doctors or healer Duke  off the streets  it isn't even mentioned that there's a  doctor shortage we then cut to dr. Hilu  Luke who is complaining that he only  managed to steal 50,000 berries and that  they should and I quote quit making such  a massive fuss over a burning house  after this Hilary Duke breaks into a  family home they beg him to leave the  dad even says that he will be getting  treatment by the other doctors here Duke  ignores this request the mother goes to  run for help naturally like any sane  person would do dr. Healy Dukes shoots  the mother with a tranquilizer knocking  her out in front of her small daughter  who is now Paul in crime he perceives to  force this unusual treatment onto the  father only to leave him in what looks  like a seizured state and that's  assuming he isn't already dead  he begins apologizing to the now  traumatized and screaming crying child  as both of her parents are now on the  floor motionless before breaking their  window and running out into the night  where he meets dr. Cudahy on the bridge  and like the rest of them now that is a  substantial difference to the animated  versions and even as the conversation  progresses with dr. CUDA  on the bridge it reveals a horrible  truth about dr. Peter Duke she says  there's nothing worse than an  incompetent physician had you not called  yourself a doctor hundreds of people  wouldn't have suffered from a simple  cold and even the direct translation I  found with the help of friends is worse  both the fan translation and ma  translation and movie translations in  this instance paint him in a much better  light so in the manga the guy is painted  to be a maniac and because Oda wanted me  to sympathize and see him as a good guy  character the entire flashback sequence  was hurt as a result for me thankfully  however because AJ supplied me with a 30  minutes section from the film to watch I  was able to really enjoy both the  character of dr. Healy Duke and  especially chopper who this entire  flashback is for capped off perfectly  with a tragically sweet swerve  incorporating poisonous mushrooms and  the most innocent and tragic of mistakes  by chopper which automatically really  wrapped up and encapsulate the entire  story's message in philosophy with dr.  Hilu whose philosophy still ringing in  the little guy's ear motivating him to  become a doctor that can cure anything  which is an inspiring thought in all I  found this whole experience with gem  Island particularly funny as in the past  I've gone back to watch certain scenes  to see how they play out in the anime  for both my own enjoyment and research  and almost every time I preferred the  experience I had in the manga but this  was one of the only times it was  different and I'm happy I had friends to  point me to spoiler free alternatives  overall I thought the axe this week were  extremely ambitious and I'm glad they  were this was our very first outing into  the Grand Line and with it we've been  exposed to it's crazy navigation  problems its geography through the use  of jungle and winter biomes we've been  introduced to a massive criminal  underworld with its incredibly  intimidating leader we've seen some  fantastic fights learned quite a lot  about how important a character  introduction is in my experiences with  the series and we've also got a new crew  mate in chopper I can't wait to see  what's to come for this ace character  and how luffy knows him but I am  chomping at the bit trying to finish  this script so that I have an excuse to  go and read the alabaster arc that  follows this this is going to be an  incredible adventure and I can't wait to  share it with all of you as always I've  been totally not mark thank you so much  for watching and I'll see you all Nick  [Music]
 

